course: dictionary
lesson: 14471
title: Partition
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To partition means to split something into parts. Partition can be a noun or a verb. «The office has a thin partition across the middle of the room.» Here a partition is a thin wall or screen that divides the room into two parts.
explain: In maths, partitioning splits a number into parts, often by place value. «To add 47 and 25, partition 47 into 40 + 7.» Now you can add 40 + 25 = 65, then 7 more, which makes 72.
explain: The same number can be partitioned in different ways. 47 can be 30 + 17 or 40 + 7, and each split still makes 47. Choose the split that makes your sum easiest.
fact: Partition means to divide something into parts.
fact: In maths, «partition» 345 means 300 + 40 + 5. Each part is worth a place value: hundreds, tens and ones.
fact: Partitioning can make adding and multiplying easier, because you work with smaller, friendlier numbers.
fact: A partition can also be a thin wall or screen that divides a room, such as in an office.
fact: In history, the partition of a country means it was split into separate countries.
exercise: «The office has a thin partition across the middle of the room.» What is a partition here? | A chair | A large cupboard | A window in the roof | *A thin wall that divides the room
exercise: Which is 46 partitioned by place value? | 6 × 40 | *40 + 6 | 46 + 0 | 4 + 6
exercise: «We partitioned the class into three groups.» What does partitioned mean here? | Counted once | Joined together | *Divided into parts | Painted
test: Which is 345 split by place value? | 340 + 5 | 300 + 40 + 5 | 3 + 4 + 5 | *300 + 40 + 5 is correct only if the parts add to 345
test: Which sentence uses partition as a verb? | The partition is grey. | A partition divides the room. | The partition is in the office. | *We partition the cake into eight slices.
exam: What do we call a country split into separate countries? | A parcel | A pattern | *A partition | A sum
